  "body": "In particular, it doesn't say anything.  Which means that I think the following things happen per spec as written right now:\n1. If the document was loaded over the network, its origin changes to the origin of its new address (which is set based on the entry settings object's responsible document).  This may or may not match the actual origin of the entry settings object's responsible document.  I believe this is clearly wrong.  Consider the case when a document at `http://foo.com` has an iframe also loaded from `http://foo.com` and a second iframe loaded from `about:blank`.  The second iframe has script injected which does a `document.open` on the first one.  This resets the address of the first one to `about:blank` and since its origin is an alias for the origin of its address, this makes its origin a new globally unique identifier, as far as I can tell.  This does not match UA behavior.\n2. If the document `open()` is called on is `about:blank` then its origin is just not related to its address and not affected at all by the `open()` call.  Similar for `javascript:` result documents, srcdoc, etc.\n\nThis inconsistency is pretty weird, from my point of view, to say nothing of the not matching UA behavior bit.\n",
